<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhys ap Gruffydd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1132–1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as The Lord Rhys, the Lord Rhys</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rhys ap Gruffydd, known as the Lord Rhys, was ruler of Deheubarth in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from 1155 to 1197. A grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a>, he recovered much of the kingdom from the Normans and reached an understanding with Henry II, who made him justiciar of south Wales. He held a festival of poetry and music at Cardigan in 1176, regarded as the first eisteddfod, and patronised the abbeys of Strata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and Talley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-gruffydd.jpg" alt="Rhys ap Gruffydd" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tipcake · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delw_fedd_Rhys_ap_Gruffudd,_Tyddewi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
